notes='''### 042-N1 — Continuation of the lemma

Clause (c) describes closed sets by a pair (B,A_u), with the boundary of A_u in T contained in B. Clause (d) describes the associated preorder when both initial topologies are ultra topological. Clause (e) introduces the ordered quotients I,I′, the closed part J, the canonical map and the quotient topologies. The last sentence continues onto the next page. Manuscript page 41; no date.

### 042-N2 — Ink decisions and literal mismatches

The first order case has τ′ and no zero: the crop leaves blank paper below and to the right. The main inequality does have a separate prime, which the enlarged crop confirms. The mathematical question about a possible τ′_0 therefore does not supply an absent index. A small isolated a-like mark remains in the right note. The two topologies after “associés à” are heavily retraced τ and τ′, not overbarred ones; detached bars are clearly different below. The final restriction is literally bar τ restricted to J′, although bar τ is located on I in the diagram. The phrase “la top de τ′ déduite de τ′” likewise retains its actual topology symbol, not an inferred I′.

### 042-N3 — Targeted check of the intended order description

Use the closed sets A∪B established on the preceding pages and the convention x≤y when x belongs to the closure of y. For y in U, every closed A∪B containing y contains y through A; thus x≤′y holds exactly when x≤τy. For y in T, the new closed sets B⊂T show that x must lie in T and that the condition is the τ′_0 order there; conversely all traces (A∩T)∪B are τ′_0-closed. No x in U can lie below y in T, since T is closed. This verifies the intended three-case description. Its first printed-on-the-sheet case with τ′ is self-referential but retained. The quotient least-fine assertion is recorded here, not separately proved.'''
